<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  "Critique the AI" exercises for MATH 53.

  Each exercise presents a plausible-looking but flawed solution attributed to
  an AI assistant. The student's job is to find the error, explain why the
  reasoning breaks, and give a correct account. The deliverable is the
  critique, which makes these resistant to being solved *by* an AI: a student
  who pastes the problem into a chatbot still has to judge whether the
  chatbot's critique is itself correct.

  Not yet wired into the book's table of contents — include it where you like
  with:
      <xi:include href="./ai-critique-exercises.ptx" />
  (add the xinclude namespace to the parent element if it isn't already there).
-->
<section xml:id="sec-ai-critique" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Find the Flaw: Critiquing an AI's Reasoning</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Each problem below shows a solution written by an AI assistant. Every one
      <em>looks</em> reasonable and reaches a confident conclusion, but each
      contains a genuine error in reasoning <mdash /> not a typo, but a mistaken
      idea. For each, do three things:
      <ol>
        <li><p>Identify the first step where the reasoning goes wrong.</p></li>
        <li><p>Explain <em>why</em> it is wrong, in your own words.</p></li>
        <li><p>Give a correct argument or a specific counterexample.</p></li>
      </ol>
      Catching a confident, well-written mistake is a different skill from
      solving from scratch <mdash /> and a more important one.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="crit-parallel-lines">
    <title>Does &#8220;No Solution&#8221; Mean Parallel?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>An AI assistant was asked when a system of linear equations in two
      variables has no solution. It replied:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          A system of linear equations in two variables is inconsistent
          <em>if and only if</em> two of its lines are parallel. Here is why:
          each equation is a line, and a solution is a point on every line. If
          two of the lines are parallel they never meet, so there is no common
          point and the system has no solution. Conversely, if no two lines are
          parallel, then every pair of lines crosses somewhere, so the system
          must have a solution.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>The final sentence <mdash /> the &#8220;conversely&#8221; <mdash /> is
      false. Find a specific system (or a picture) that breaks it, and explain
      the mistake in the reasoning.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The converse fails. Take three lines that form a triangle <mdash /> for
        instance <m>x_2 = 0</m>, <m>x_2 = x_1</m>, and <m>x_2 = -x_1 + 2</m>. No
        two are parallel, yet the three cross pairwise at three
        <em>different</em> points <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(1,1)</m>, and <m>(2,0)</m>,
        so no single point lies on all three and the system is inconsistent.
      </p>
      <p>
        The flaw is that &#8220;every pair meets&#8221; only guarantees pairwise
        intersection points; it does not force those points to coincide. A
        common solution must be a single point shared by <em>all</em> the lines
        at once, which is a much stronger condition once there are more than two
        equations. (The forward direction is also loosely stated: two lines that
        coincide are sometimes called parallel, yet such a system can be
        consistent <mdash /> the careful statement is &#8220;two distinct
        parallel lines.&#8221;)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="crit-zero-row">
    <title>A Row of Zeros</title>
    <statement>
      <p>An AI assistant row-reduced the augmented matrix of the system
      <md>
        <mrow>x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp = 3</mrow>
        <mrow>2x_1 + 2x_2 + 2x_3 \amp = 6</mrow>
        <mrow>x_1 - x_2 \amp = 0</mrow>
      </md>
      and wrote:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          Subtracting twice the first equation from the second turns the second
          row into <m>0 = 0</m>, that is <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0
          &amp; 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. A row of all zeros cannot be satisfied by
          any choice of variables, so this equation is impossible. Therefore the
          system is inconsistent and has no solution.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>The conclusion is wrong. What did the assistant misread, and how many
      solutions does the system actually have?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The row <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
        encodes the equation <m>0 = 0</m>, which is <em>always</em> true, not
        impossible. It simply means the second equation carried no new
        information (it was twice the first). The assistant confused it with a
        row like <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        meaning <m>0 = 5</m> <mdash /> <em>that</em> is the impossible one that
        signals inconsistency.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the redundant equation is dropped, two genuine equations remain in
        three unknowns, leaving a free variable, so the system has
        <em>infinitely many</em> solutions. (Solving: <m>x_1 = x_2</m> from the
        third equation, and then <m>2x_1 + x_3 = 3</m>, so
        <m>x_1 = x_2 = t</m>, <m>x_3 = 3 - 2t</m> for any <m>t</m>.)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="crit-independence-count">
    <title>&#8220;None Is a Multiple of Another&#8221;</title>
    <statement>
      <p>An AI assistant was asked whether the vectors
      <m>\mathbf{v}_1 = (1,2)</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}_2 = (3,1)</m>, and
      <m>\mathbf{v}_3 = (2,5)</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> are linearly
      independent. It answered:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          Check each pair: <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is not a scalar multiple of
          <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is not a scalar multiple of
          <m>\mathbf{v}_3</m>, and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> is not a scalar multiple
          of <m>\mathbf{v}_3</m>. Since no vector is a multiple of another, none
          is redundant, so the three vectors are linearly independent.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>The answer is wrong. Explain why the test used is not enough, and
      exhibit an actual linear dependence among the three vectors.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        &#8220;No vector is a scalar multiple of another&#8221; only rules out
        dependence <em>within pairs</em>; independence of the whole set is a
        stronger condition. In fact any three vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> are
        automatically dependent, because you cannot have more independent
        vectors than the dimension of the space.
      </p>
      <p>
        Concretely, solve <m>a\mathbf{v}_1 + b\mathbf{v}_2 = \mathbf{v}_3</m>:
        <m>a + 3b = 2</m> and <m>2a + b = 5</m> give <m>a = \tfrac{13}{5}</m>,
        <m>b = -\tfrac{1}{5}</m>. Thus
        <me>\tfrac{13}{5}\,\mathbf{v}_1 - \tfrac{1}{5}\,\mathbf{v}_2 - \mathbf{v}_3 = \mathbf{0},</me>
        a nontrivial dependence, so the set is linearly dependent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="crit-homogeneous">
    <title>The Zero Solution</title>
    <statement>
      <p>An AI assistant was asked how many solutions a homogeneous system
      <m>A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}</m> has. It said:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The zero vector always satisfies <m>A\mathbf{0} = \mathbf{0}</m>, so
          <m>\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}</m> is a solution. And the zero vector is
          unique. Therefore every homogeneous system has exactly one solution,
          the trivial one.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>The first sentence is true but the conclusion is false. Where is the
      leap, and when does a homogeneous system have more than one solution?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The error is sliding from &#8220;the zero <em>vector</em> is
        unique&#8221; to &#8220;the <em>solution</em> is unique.&#8221; Those are
        different claims. The zero vector is always <em>a</em> solution, so a
        homogeneous system is never inconsistent, but there can be many other,
        nonzero solutions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nontrivial solutions appear exactly when the row-reduced system has a
        free variable <mdash /> for example whenever there are more unknowns than
        pivots (in particular, more unknowns than equations). The system
        <m>x_1 - x_2 = 0</m> alone has the whole line
        <m>(t, t)</m> as solutions. A homogeneous system has the trivial
        solution as its <em>only</em> solution precisely when the columns of
        <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="crit-span">
    <title>Independent, Therefore Spanning?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>An AI assistant was asked whether <m>(1,0,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,0)</m> span
      <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. It replied:</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          These two vectors are linearly independent, since neither is a scalar
          multiple of the other. Linearly independent vectors always span the
          space they live in. Therefore <m>(1,0,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,0)</m> span
          <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>The vectors really are independent, but the conclusion is false. Which
      sentence is the culprit, and what vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> is
      <em>not</em> in their span?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The false sentence is &#8220;linearly independent vectors always span the
        space.&#8221; Independence and spanning are separate properties:
        independence says the vectors are not redundant, spanning says there are
        enough of them to reach every point. A basis needs <em>both</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every combination <m>a(1,0,0) + b(0,1,0) = (a, b, 0)</m> has third
        coordinate <m>0</m>, so the span is only the <m>x_1x_2</m>-plane. The
        vector <m>(0,0,1)</m> is not in the span. Spanning
        <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> requires three independent vectors, not two.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</section>
